  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Prove that $1\\,000\\,001$ is composite no matter what base you’re working in.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "In base $b\\geq2$, $(1000001)_b=(b^2+1)(b^4-b^2+1)$. Both factors exceed $1$, so the number is composite.",
      "worked": "Let the base be an integer $b\\geq2$. By place value and the sum-of-cubes identity,\n$$\\begin{align*}(1000001)_b&=b^6+1\\\\(1000001)_b&=(b^2)^3+1^3\\\\(1000001)_b&=(b^2+1)(b^4-b^2+1).\\end{align*}$$\nHere $b^2+1\\geq5$ and $b^4-b^2+1=b^2(b^2-1)+1\\geq13$. Both factors are integers greater than $1$, so the number is composite in every base. The factor $b^2+1$ is written $101$ in base $b$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
